2026-08-20 - Host Agent heartbeat recovery and live-preflight fallback
- Hardened Host Agent heartbeat persistence against Docker PostgreSQL restarts by using bounded, short-lived PostgreSQL heartbeat connections instead of allowing a stale long-lived telemetry connection to strand the heartbeat loop. Failed heartbeat persistence is rate-limited to one outage warning, automatically retried on the normal heartbeat cadence, and emits a recovery message when PostgreSQL becomes reachable again.
- Added `SKYCOMMAND_HOST_AGENT_HEARTBEAT_DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS` with a 3000 ms default so host-side heartbeat recovery fails fast while Docker PostgreSQL is restarting instead of hanging the Host Agent telemetry loop.
- Upgraded workflow Host Agent preflight to use the persisted heartbeat ledger as the fast primary signal and a bounded live Temporal Host Agent health workflow as the fallback whenever the heartbeat is stale or unavailable. A reachable Host Agent can therefore remain workflow-ready during transient telemetry recovery, while disabled or genuinely unreachable host execution still fails closed.
- Shortened the internal Host Agent health activity schedule/start timeouts while retaining the existing 45-second/10-minute production Local Repository Sync activity bounds.
- Expanded Host Agent and workflow-preflight regression coverage and documented the Docker-restart resilience proof. No database migration or workflow-definition migration is required; rebuild the Docker API/Temporal worker and restart the host-native Host Agent to activate the new code.

2026-08-19 - Host Agent Scheduled Task native-stderr correction
- Fixed the Windows automatic-start Host Agent runner so routine Temporal lifecycle output written to native stderr cannot be promoted into a terminating Windows PowerShell 5.1 error while `$ErrorActionPreference='Stop'` is active. Strict wrapper error handling remains in place; Node stdout/stderr continue to append to the scheduled-task log and the native Node exit code remains authoritative.
- Hardened `host-agent:auto-start:status` to distinguish Task Scheduler state from actual Host Agent process state, report detected PIDs and the log path, and return a failing status when the automatic worker is not actually running.
- Extended automatic-start regression coverage and Host Agent setup guidance. No database migration, workflow reconfiguration, or Docker rebuild is required; reinstall the Scheduled Task only if its registration itself changes.

2026-08-19 - Host Agent operational hardening
- Added workflow start preflight for host-execution nodes. SkyCommand now refuses to queue workflows that require the Host Agent when `SKYCOMMAND_HOST_AGENT_ENABLED` is false or when the dedicated host task queue has no recent Host Agent heartbeat, returning explicit disabled/unavailable diagnostics before any workflow run begins.
- Added a seventh Command Center Platform Availability card for the host-native SkyCommand Host Agent, backed by the existing Temporal worker heartbeat ledger and able to distinguish disabled, online, stale, and offline states.
- Reinforced the Development Promotion result contract with regression coverage: remote synchronization without proven four-way host convergence remains `REMOTE_PROMOTED`; only successful Local Repository Sync proof upgrades the rollup to `PROMOTED`.
- Added native Windows automatic startup for the Host Agent through a limited-privilege per-user Scheduled Task with restart-on-failure, duplicate-instance protection, dedicated log capture, install/status/start/stop/uninstall npm helpers, and regression coverage.

2026-08-19 - Development Promotion Host Agent workflow integration
- Added migration `00101__development_promotion_host_sync_node.sql`, which publishes a new immutable `skyserver_dev_commit` workflow version and inserts `local_repo_sync_node` between `merge_sync_node` and `dev_promotion_summary` without rewriting historical workflow graphs. Existing drafts block the migration so unreviewed workflow edits are never silently published or discarded.
- Bound Local Repository Sync directly to `{{ params.repoName }}`, trusted Dev Commit `{{ nodes.dev_commit_node.output.currentHeadSha }}`, and Remote Merge `{{ nodes.merge_sync_node.output.synchronizedHeadSha }}`. The node runs once, routes through the existing Temporal Host Agent transport, and fails closed if four-way host synchronization cannot be proven.
- Added `npm run development-promotion:host-sync:check` to verify the published graph, canonical bindings, edge replacement, tool enablement, and CLI/Admin-Web/API/worker visibility before a live big-rig run.
- Updated Host Agent and README documentation so a successful Development Promotion now means remote promotion plus guarded host convergence, allowing the structured summary to finish as fully `PROMOTED`.

2026-08-19 - SkyCommand Host Agent workflow bridge
- Added an activity-only SkyCommand Host Agent that runs on the repository-owning host, polls the dedicated `skycommand-host-local` Temporal task queue, refuses Docker/DOCKER_LOCAL execution, validates host repository paths before polling, and records Temporal worker heartbeats with HOST_AGENT metadata.
- Upgraded `local_repo_sync` into a dual transport: host CLI execution keeps the existing guarded implementation, while Docker/API/workflow execution fails closed unless Host Agent dispatch is enabled and then starts a short Temporal bridge workflow that routes only the allow-listed local-sync activity to the host.
- Added migration `00100__host_agent_local_repository_sync.sql` to expose Local Repository Sync to Admin-Web/API/worker workflow execution without exposing arbitrary host shell access; the container executes only the Temporal bridge while the host owns all `.git` mutation.
- Added `npm run host-agent`, `host-agent:dev`, and `host-agent:check`, plus an end-to-end health probe, regression coverage, and setup documentation. Development Promotion can now place Local Repository Sync after Remote Merge and bind the trusted Dev Commit `currentHeadSha` plus Remote Merge `synchronizedHeadSha` directly from workflow context.

2026-08-19 - Guarded host local repository synchronization foundation
- Split Docker Development Promotion semantics into authoritative remote promotion and host-owned local synchronization: DOCKER_LOCAL Main Merge now reports `localHostSyncRequired`, deferred local branches, and a host-sync command template instead of implying that Windows refs were updated.
- Added the CLI-only `local_repo_sync` tool and `git_local_sync_summary.v1` contract. The host action requires the trusted Dev Commit `currentHeadSha` plus the exact Remote Merge `synchronizedHeadSha`, acquires a repository lock, blocks dirty/in-progress/multi-worktree/conflicting state, detects local commits made after the trusted baseline, re-verifies the remote immediately before mutation, permits fast-forward-only changes, and requires local main/dev plus origin/main/dev to converge on the exact approved SHA.
- Added migration `00099__local_repository_sync_tool.sql` with SUPER_ADMIN-only permission, three explicit parameters, CLI-only visibility, and future Host Agent/Temporal compatibility without exposing arbitrary host shell execution.
- Development Promotion rollups now distinguish `REMOTE_PROMOTED` from fully `PROMOTED`, expose copy-ready local-sync inputs/command, and render dedicated remote/host synchronization evidence in Tool/Workflow Operations.

2026-08-18 - Docker Main Merge initial-fetch isolation and full-stack restart helper
- Closed the remaining DOCKER_LOCAL Git ref-lock race revealed after host-ref isolation: Main Merge no longer starts with `git fetch --prune origin` inside the Linux container, because that command can still rewrite Windows-owned `refs/remotes/origin/*` before the remote-only synchronization logic runs.
- DOCKER_LOCAL now resolves authoritative main/dev heads with `git ls-remote`, transfers only the required commit/tree objects using `git fetch --no-tags --no-write-fetch-head` (no remote-tracking ref or FETCH_HEAD update), disables optional Git locks for container-side read operations, and continues to leave host-owned local branch refs untouched.
- Added `npm run skycommand:docker:restart`; consistent with the existing per-service restart helpers, it rebuilds and force-recreates the complete six-container SkyCommand runtime so code changes baked into Docker images are actually activated while PostgreSQL/Temporal persistent volumes remain intact.
- Updated Docker regression guards and README command documentation. No database migration or workflow reconfiguration is required.

2026-08-18 - Docker Main Merge host-ref isolation
- Fixed the Development Promotion Main Merge false failure seen after full Docker cutover: the remote main -> dev fast-forward had already succeeded, but the Linux Temporal worker then failed while force-updating `refs/heads/main` inside the Windows-hosted bind-mounted repository.
- DOCKER_LOCAL Main Merge now pushes through the configured remote URL so Git does not opportunistically rewrite the local `refs/remotes/origin/dev` tracking ref, verifies authoritative remote heads with `git ls-remote`, and intentionally leaves host-owned local branch refs untouched.
- When the checked-out host branch is behind the synchronized remote, the successful ToolResult now reports a local refresh requirement and preserves the existing safe `git switch dev && git pull --ff-only origin dev` follow-up instead of turning a completed remote synchronization into a failed workflow node.
- DEV_LOCAL behavior is unchanged: host-run Main Merge may continue updating local branch references checkout-free when safe. No database migration or workflow reconfiguration is required.

2026-08-18 - Fixed PostgreSQL shadow parity false negatives across Windows-to-Linux migration by canonicalizing TIMESTAMPTZ JSON rendering to UTC before row hashing; parity diagnostics now report source/candidate server time zones so restored data is compared by stored instant rather than session-local timestamp text.
2026-08-17 - PostgreSQL pre-cutover workflow catalogue query correction
- Fixed the Docker PostgreSQL parity and SkyCommand_Core candidate checks to read `published_version_id` from `worker.vw_workflow_definitions`, where publication metadata is derived from workflow versions, instead of incorrectly querying the base `worker.workflow_definitions` table.
- Aligned the candidate Core workflow count with the actual CLI catalogue rules by requiring active, enabled, Admin-visible definitions with a published version.
- Added regression assertions that preserve the base-table fingerprints while requiring publication-aware summary/check queries to use the workflow catalogue view.
